  word while can be used with both a  contrastive meaning and with a  circumstantial meaning so it might be a  good alternative translation for these  words meaning and in demonstrating these  similarities I've been using Semitic  languages but the same similarities can  be found in other languages in other  branches  of the afro-asiatic language family not  only the Semitic branch languages like  amazeen also known as Berber and ancient  Egyptian also correspond in similar ways  now the question is why do those  similarities exist between insular  Celtic languages and Semitic and  afro-asiatic languages they could just  be a set of typological similarities  meaning that these features arose  similarly but independently of each  other and not as a result of contact the  other possibility of course is that they  arose as a result of contact celtic  languages now are mostly limited to  Great Britain and Ireland as well as  Brittany in France but in the past they  were all over Europe including the  Iberian Peninsula right across from  Africa and including Asia Minor right  around present-day Ankara Turkey not too  far from the Levant to the south that  means that there were significant  chances of contact with afro-asiatic  languages but some linguists theories go  back further than that and they suggest  that before Indo Europeans arrived in  Western Europe much of the area was  populated by speakers of an early  afro-asiatic language and later they  adopted indo-european languages while on  the continent the number of  indo-europeans was large enough that the  earlier languages didn't leave much of  an influence in Ireland and the British  Isles the population of pre  indo-europeans was large enough that  their speech patterns influenced the  insular Celtic languages as they  developed so the theory is that the  insular Celtic languages have an  afro-asiatic substrate such theories are  inconclusive and unproven but are they  far-fetched  not really we know that continuous human  settlement in Ireland and Great Britain  began thousands of years before the  arrival of the celts and we know that  while there were hunter-gatherers in  europe already early farmers began  migrating to Europe from the Near East  Anatolia and the Levant during the  Neolithic period one theory the  Anatolian theory suggests that these  farmers brought indo-european with them  but it's more widely believed that  indo-europeans arrived later during the  Chaco lithic and bronze periods if  that's the case what kind of language  did the farmers migrating from the Near  East speak it's possible that they  spoken afro-asiatic language one  linguist Theo van Amman has the  rised that between 5,000 and 2500 BCE  seafaring people's whom he refers to as  paleo Phoenicians spread the megalithic  culture of large stone structures all  the way up the Atlantic coast to Ireland  the British Isles and beyond and brought  their afro-asiatic language with them he  refers to this language or these  languages as somatic since they may have  belonged to a branch of afro-asiatic  that was close to semitic but has since  died off except as a substratum in  indo-european languages  Theo venneman goes even further to  suggest that SEMATECH languages are the  source of some non-indo-european  vocabulary in Germanic languages again  many linguists have challenged his  theories and emphasized their  speculative nature the truth is that we  don't know exactly what kind of  languages were spoken in prehistoric  times or what kind of contact they had  with each other what we do know is that  the insular Celtic languages are  curiously different from other  indo-european languages in certain ways  and in those same ways they seem similar  to Semitic and afro-asiatic languages  beyond that imagining how that situation  came about is merely a fascinating  exercise the question of the day for  speakers of Celtic languages and Semitic  or afro-asiatic languages are you  surprised by these similarities between  the two groups and for other people do  your languages share any of the features  that Celtic and afro-asiatic languages  have in common if you enjoyed this video  be sure to check out the various lengths  okis social media accounts like Facebook  YouTube and Instagram and once again  thank you to all of my wonderful patreon  supporters especially these people right  here on the screen who are my top tier  patreon supporters so many extra special  thanks to them and to everyone thank you  for watching and have a nice day  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

